<exercise xml:id="dehumidifier-sine-models">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Sara and her brother Nahiz run a small coffee shop, Qamaria Cafe. The basement serves as storage and tends to be fairly humid so they run a dehumidifier to remove water from the air. The dehumidifier automatically cycles on and off. When it turns off, the humidity rises a bit and when it turns back on, the humidity falls again. The humidity (<m>H</m>%) depends on the time (<m>T</m> hours) where we measure time from 12:00 PM (midnight) when the machine turns off. An equation describing this dependence is
            <md>
                H = 10\sin(90T)+45
            </md>
            where the sine function is set to take inputs in degrees.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                According to the equation, what is the humidity level at midnight? Hint: <m>T = 0</m>. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Make a table showing the humidity at midnight, 1:00 AM, 2:00 AM, 3:00 AM, 4:00 AM, 5:00 AM, 6:00 AM, 7:00 AM and 8:00 AM. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Draw a graph illustrating the relationship.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What is the average humidity level? Hint: it's the height of the midline of the graph. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                How often does the humidifier make a full cycle (on/off)?  Hint: it's the period.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="latte-revenue-sine-models">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Sara's specialty is lattes <ndash/> hot espresso coffee with steamed milk. Qamaria Cafe's monthly latte revenue goes up and down depending on the season. Based on data from 2025, Sara estimates that
            <md>
                R=6000\sin(30M)+8000
            </md>
            where
            <md>
                M = \text{ time (in months since October 2025) }\sim \text{ indep}
            </md>
            <md>
                R = \text{ latte revenue (in \$) }\sim \text{ dep}
            </md>
            and the sine function is set to take inputs in degrees.
        </p>

        <p>
            Nahiz drew a graph showing three years of expected revenue if Sara's model continues.
        </p>
        <image>
            <latex-image xml:id="tikz-7-4-latte-revenue">
            \begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=0.2333, yscale=0.35]
                % Grid lines (horizontal)
                \draw[gray!20, line width=0.4pt] (0,2) -- (36,2);
                \draw[gray!20, line width=0.4pt] (0,4) -- (36,4);
                \draw[gray!20, line width=0.4pt] (0,6) -- (36,6);
                \draw[gray!20, line width=0.4pt] (0,8) -- (36,8);
                \draw[gray!20, line width=0.4pt] (0,10) -- (36,10);
                \draw[gray!20, line width=0.4pt] (0,12) -- (36,12);
                \draw[gray!20, line width=0.4pt] (0,14) -- (36,14);

                % Grid lines (vertical)
                \foreach \x in {0, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21, 24, 27, 30, 33, 36} {
                    \draw[gray!20, line width=0.4pt] (\x,0) -- (\x,14);
                }

                % Axes
                \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,0) -- (37.5,0) node[below right] {$M$};
                \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,0) -- (0,15.5) node[above left] {$R$};

                % Y-axis ticks and labels
                \foreach \y/\label in {2/2000, 4/4000, 6/6000, 8/8000, 10/10000, 12/12000, 14/14000} {
                    \draw (0.3,\y) -- (-0.3,\y) node[left] {\small\label};
                }
                \node[left] at (-0.3,0) {\small 0};

                % X-axis ticks and labels (3 years / 36 months)
                \foreach \x in {0, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21, 24, 27, 30, 33, 36} {
                    \draw (\x, 0.25) -- (\x, -0.25) node[below] {\small\x};
                }

                % Plot the Sine Curve R = 6*sin(30*M) + 8
                \draw[smooth, ultra thick, blue!80!black, domain=0:36, samples=200] 
                    plot (\x, {6*sin(30*\x) + 8});
            \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                According to the equation, what is the latte revenue in October 2026?  Indicate the point on the graph that shows the answer.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                According to the graph, what is the highest latte revenue and in which month of the year does it occur? Explain why your answer makes sense.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                According to the graph, what is the lowest latte revenue and in which month does it occur? Explain why your answer makes sense. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                According to the graph, what is the period of this sine model and what does it mean in the story? 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                How could we calculate the period of this sine model from the equation?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If we were to include iced latte sales, how do you think the graph would change?  Note: iced lattes are lovely when it's hot out! 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="piston-sine-models">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Inside a car's engine, controlled micro-explosions of gasoline push a metal cylinder called a piston up and down. The piston's movement turns these explosions into rotational force that powers the vehicle's wheels. When a car is parked with its engine running (idling) the height, <m>H</m> cm, of the middle of the piston (above the base) is given by the equation
            <md>
                H=4 \sin(4500S) + 10
            </md>
            where <m>S</m> is the time since the car started, measured in seconds, and the sine function is set to take inputs in degrees.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Make a table showing the height of the middle of the piston after 0, 0.02, 0.04, 0.06, 0.08, 0.10, 0.12, 0.14, 0.16, 0.18, and 0.20 seconds.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use the equation to find the period and explain what it means in the story.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Draw a graph showing the height of the piston for the first 0.20 seconds.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What does the constant 10 mean in the story and what are its units? 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What does the 4 mean in the story and what are its units?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Indicate where we can see 10, 4, and 0.08 on the graph. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The car's engine runs at 0.08 seconds per cycle.  Convert units to see that the car's engine runs at 12.5 cycles per second.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Convert units to calculate the RPM, which is revolutions (or cycles) per minute.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="tides-sine-models">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Ocean tides force seawater to flow back and forth through a narrow opening into a bay. At midnight on Monday, the water reached <q>slack tide</q>, momentarily stopping before it began flowing inward. The water's speed (measured in knots) changes continuously: positive values mean water is rushing into the bay, while negative values mean water is flowing out toward the ocean. The water reaches a maximum inward speed of <m>+4\text{ knots}</m>, drops back to <m>0\text{ knots}</m> at slack tide, reaches a peak outward speed of <m>-4\text{ knots}</m>, and then returns to <m>0\text{ knots}</m> to repeat the process. This complete back-and-forth cycle repeats every 12 hours and 25 minutes.
        </p>

        <p>
            This water flow can be modeled with a sine equation where
            <md>
                H = \text{time (hours since midnight)}\sim \text{indep}
            </md>
            <md>
                S = \text{water's speed (knots)}\sim \text{dep}
            </md>
            and the sine function is set to take inputs in degrees.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                According to the story, what is the midline of the sine model?  Hint: it occurs at the slack tide.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                According to the story, what is the amplitude of the sine model?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                According to the story, what is the period of the sine model? 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Convert 12 hours and 25 minutes to decimal hours.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Write an equation showing how the water's speed varies over time. Hint: You will need to use the number 360 and your answer to (d) to find the constant multiplying <m>H</m> in the equation.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="food-bank-sine-models" component="not-workbook">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            A community food bank experiences seasonal shifts in weekly volunteer hours. Starting on October 1, volunteer participation begins to pick up, rising to a high around January 1 then dipping to its lowest values around July 1. Volunteer participation <m>V</m> hours/week is a function of the time of year, <m>M</m> measured in months since October 1. It can be modeled with a sine function whose inputs are in degrees. The equation is
            <md>
                V = 70 \sin(30M) + 150
            </md>
            Here's a graph of this function for three years.
        </p>
        <image>
            <latex-image xml:id="tikz-7-4-food-bank">
            \begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=0.22, yscale=0.018]
                % Vertical Graph Paper Lines (Every 3 months from M = 0 to 36)
                \foreach \x in {0, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21, 24, 27, 30, 33, 36} {
                    \draw[gray!30, thin] (\x, 0) -- (\x, 240);
                }

                % Horizontal Graph Paper Lines (At all specified V values)
                \foreach \y in {25, 50, 75, 100, 125, 150, 175, 200, 225} {
                    \draw[gray!30, thin] (0, \y) -- (36, \y);
                }
                
                % Main Axes
                \draw[-stealth, thick] (-1,0) -- (38,0) node[right] {$M$};
                \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,-10) -- (0,255) node[above] {$V$};
                
                % X-axis Ticks &amp; Labels (Every 3 months)
                \foreach \x in {0, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21, 24, 27, 30, 33, 36} {
                    \draw (\x, 3) -- (\x, -3) node[below, font=\small] {\x};
                }
                
                % Y-axis Ticks &amp; Labels (Including 25, 75, 125, 175, 225)
                \foreach \y in {25, 50, 75, 100, 125, 150, 175, 200, 225} {
                    \draw (0.3, \y) -- (-0.3, \y) node[left, font=\small] {\y};
                }
                
                % Sine Curve for 36 months (3 full cycles)
                % Equation: V = 70*sin(30*M) + 150
                \draw[very thick, blue!80!black, smooth, domain=0:36, samples=200]
                    plot (\x, {70*sin(30*\x) + 150});
                    
                % Key Points Highlighted (Peaks, Troughs, Midline points for Year 1)
                \foreach \x/\y in {0/150, 3/220, 6/150, 9/80, 12/150} {
                    \fill[blue!80!black] (\x, \y) circle (8pt);
                }
            \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use the equation to calculate the volunteer participation at <m>M = 0</m>, <m>M = 3</m>, <m>M = 6</m>, <m>M = 9</m>, and <m>M = 12</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                According to the graph, what's the highest level of volunteer participation and in what month does that occur? 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use your equation to determine the highest level of volunteer participation.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                According to the graph, what's the lowest level of volunteer participation and in what month does that occur? 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use your equation to determine the lowest level of volunteer participation.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                According to the graph, what's the average number of volunteer hours?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use your equation to determine the average level of volunteer participation.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="dock-sensor-sine-models" component="not-workbook">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            On the shore of a large lake, a sensor on a dock measures the water height. As lake waves pass through, the water level rises above and drops below the average. An equation modeling this phenomenon is
            <md>
                W = 2.5 \sin(60T) + 12
            </md>
            where
            <md>
                W = \text{water height (in feet)}\sim \text{dep}
            </md>
            <md>
                T = \text{time (in seconds)}\sim \text{indep}
            </md>
            and the sine function is set to take inputs in degrees.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What does the constant 12 mean in the story and what are its units?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What does the 2.5 mean in the story and what are its units?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                How fast do the waves come? Hint: compute the period. Don't forget the units.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Draw a graph showing the water height for the first minute. Hint: that means up to 60 seconds.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Explain how to see 12, 2.5, and the period in the graph.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="warehouse-sine-models" component="not-workbook">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            A warehouse maintains an average inventory of <m>500\text{ units}</m>. Deliveries and demand cause stock to fluctuate smoothly between a maximum capacity of <m>800\text{ units}</m> and a low of <m>200\text{ units}</m>. At <m>0\text{ weeks}</m>, inventory is right at <m>500\text{ units}</m> and rising following a new shipment cycle. The inventory cycle repeats every <m>4\text{ weeks}</m>. The inventory can be modeled with a sine equation where
            <md>
                N = \text{ inventory (units) }\sim \text{ dep}
            </md>
            <md>
                W = \text{ time (weeks) }\sim \text{ indep}
            </md>
            and the sine function is set to take inputs in degrees.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               According to the story, what's the midline of the sine model? 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               According to the story, what's the amplitude of the sine model? 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               According to the story, what's the period of the sine model? 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Write an equation showing how the inventory varies over time. Hint: You will need to use the number 360 and your answer to (c) to find the constant multiplying <m>T</m> in the equation.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

</exercises>